786	// A Comment sub-block chain that outruns the read budget is decided by the
787	// chain itself: see the `None` arm above. This is the fifth attempt at that
788	// decision and the first that does not invent a discriminator, so the four
789	// refused rules are recorded here to stop a sixth from re-deriving one.
790	// Measurements are against a 939-file ground-truth corpus (674 images / 265
791	// text files, every image decode-checked twice — Pillow `open`+`load` and
792	// giflib `DGifOpenFileName`+`DGifSlurp`), plus 138 later counterexamples built
793	// specifically to break whichever rule was current.
794	//
795	//   * `false` ("inconclusive means text", round 2): refuses all 92 corpus
796	//     images that open on an outrunning comment; 88 become 3,063 prose records.
797	//   * `seen > 1 && every size seen is 0xFF` (round 3), on the reasoning that
798	//     encoders pack long comments maximally. They do not. giflib's streaming
799	//     `EGifPutExtensionBlock` writes ONE sub-block per call at whatever length
800	//     the caller passed, so chains of 1, 2, 16, 32, 64, 100, 120, 127, 128,
801	//     192, 200 and 250..=254 are ordinary encoder output; ImageMagick writes
802	//     200/199/199…, and the eight real commented GIFs on the build machine
803	//     (GIMP, ezgif.com, ajaxload.info) carry 17, 26, 45 and 49 and not one 255.
804	//     Refuses 37 of the 92.
805	//   * the canvas high bytes at offsets 7 and 9 (round 4), on the reasoning
806	//     that printable text can only declare a dimension >= 0x2000. True, and
807	//     still a guess about the file rather than a reading of it: it refuses any
808	//     real image whose canvas high byte is printable. 30 decodable GIFs
809	//     regress to `TxtProse` and 37,450 junk records, and the corpus could not
810	//     see it because its canvas high bytes are all 0..8 and 11.
811	//   * the Global Colour Table flag (`packed & 0x80`), the other LSD field that
812	//     looks unforgeable: it is not. A windows-1252 accented letter at offset 10
813	//     sets it, which is how the corpus's own `gctflag_*` fixtures were built —
814	//     8 of them are junked as images by that rule.
815	//
816	// The lesson each of those shares is that a property of the files at hand was
817	// written down as a property of the format. The budget test is not that: a
818	// full buffer means a megabyte of sub-block chain was actually traversed.
819	//
820	// HISTORICAL, and left here because the mistake is instructive. For four
821	// commits (a182317, 0e5d740, d778f70, cf60b65) the short-buffer answer was
822	// plain `prefix.len() >= GIF_PREFIX`, and
823	// this paragraph recorded its cost in the present tense: truncated GIFs, plain
824	// and gzipped alike, going to the prose extractor — 7 of 7 plain cuts, after an
825	// earlier draft had wrongly narrowed it to `.gif.gz` only. All true then.
826	//
827	// It stopped being true when the canvas fallback came back, and the arm now
828	// says so. The paragraph nonetheless survived four commits and six reviews
829	// still asserting the opposite of the code twenty lines above it — which is
830	// the very failure it was written to warn about, one document later, in the
831	// document doing the warning.
832	//
833	// A previous version of THIS paragraph then made the same class of mistake
834	// while correcting it. It offered "13 of 13 curated cuts and 60 of 60
835	// real-corpus cuts at 300 KB / 700 KB / 1,048,575 B are Binary" as proof that
836	// the canvas fallback is what obsoleted the old rule. The cuts are Binary, but
837	// the canvas fallback decides NONE of them: at those three sizes the comment
838	// chain still terminates inside the buffer, so the walk never reaches the
839	// `None` arm at all and an earlier arm answers. Measured over the 674-image
840	// corpus, counting how many cuts reach the `None` arm and how many of those
841	// the canvas disjunct then decides:
842	//
843	//     cut        files >= cut   reach `None` arm   canvas-decided
844	//     8,192               249                 90               90
845	//     300,000              83                  0                0
846	//     700,000              61                  0                0
847	//     1,048,575            53                  0                0
848	//
849	// So the real "13 of 13" figure is an 8 KiB measurement, quoted against three
850	// sizes where the mechanism it names is inert. The canvas disjunct is
851	// load-bearing — at 8,192 it decides all 90 — but not there.
852	
853	fn canvas_dimension_text_cannot_write(prefix: &[u8]) -> bool {
854	    // `prefix[..13]` is guaranteed by the length check in the only caller.
855	    let high_byte_is_binary = |hi: u8| hi < 0x20 && !matches!(hi, b'\t' | b'\n' | b'\r');
856	    high_byte_is_binary(prefix[7]) || high_byte_is_binary(prefix[9])
857	}
